  <div class="card">
    <h3>Why a lock-free SPSC ring, and how it works</h3>
    <p>
      Each endpoint has exactly one producer and one consumer — the two processes. That lets the queue be a
      single-producer/single-consumer ring buffer with just two atomics and no lock: the producer advances
      <code class="mono">tail</code> with a Release store, the consumer advances <code class="mono">head</code>,
      and capacity is a power of two so wrapping is a bitmask.
    </p>
<pre><code><span class="c">// ipc/queue.rs — the entire synchronisation story is two atomics</span>
pub fn try_push(&amp;self, msg: Message) -&gt; Result&lt;(), QueueError&gt; &#123;
    let tail = self.tail.load(Relaxed);
    let head = self.head.load(Acquire);
    if tail - head &gt;= self.capacity as u64 &#123; return Err(QueueError::Full); &#125;
    let index = (tail &amp; self.mask as u64) as usize;
    unsafe &#123; (*self.buffer.add(index)).write(msg); &#125;
    self.tail.store(tail + 1, Release);      <span class="c">// publish the write</span>
    Ok(())
&#125;
pub fn try_pop(&amp;self) -&gt; Result&lt;Message, QueueError&gt; &#123;
    let head = self.head.load(Relaxed);
    let tail = self.tail.load(Acquire);      <span class="c">// see everything published before tail</span>
    if head == tail &#123; return if self.is_closed.load(Acquire)
        &#123; Err(QueueError::Closed) &#125; else &#123; Err(QueueError::Empty) &#125;; &#125;
    let msg = unsafe &#123; (*self.buffer.add((head &amp; self.mask as u64) as usize)).assume_init_read() &#125;;
    self.head.store(head + 1, Release);
    Ok(msg)
&#125;</code></pre>
    <p>
      The Acquire/Release pairing is the whole memory-ordering argument: the Release store to
      <code class="mono">tail</code> makes the message bytes visible before the index, and the Acquire load
      makes the consumer see them. No lock, no <code class="mono">SeqCst</code> needed for this pattern.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What a message carries</h2>
  <figure class="diagram">
    <div class="diagram-scroll">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 1000 200" role="img" aria-labelledby="msg-title msg-desc" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="width:100%">
        <title id="msg-title">Message layout</title>
        <desc id="msg-desc">A message has a header with type, sender and flags, a 256-byte inline payload, and space for four inline capability identifiers.</desc>
        <rect width="1000" height="200" fill="#f5f5f5"/>
        <rect x="60" y="60" width="240" height="70" rx="6" fill="#f5f5f5" stroke="#2d3142" stroke-width="1.2"/>
        <text x="180" y="88" fill="#2d3142" font-size="12" font-weight="600" font-family="'Geist', sans-serif" text-anchor="middle">MessageHeader</text>
        <text x="180" y="106" fill="#4f5d75" font-size="8.5" font-family="'Geist Mono', monospace" text-anchor="middle">type · flags · sender_pid</text>
        <text x="180" y="120" fill="#4f5d75" font-size="8.5" font-family="'Geist Mono', monospace" text-anchor="middle">size · cap_count · priority</text>
        <rect x="330" y="60" width="300" height="70" rx="6" fill="#f5f5f5" stroke="#2d3142" stroke-width="1.2"/>
        <text x="480" y="88" fill="#2d3142" font-size="12" font-weight="600" font-family="'Geist', sans-serif" text-anchor="middle">inline_payload</text>
        <text x="480" y="106" fill="#4f5d75" font-size="8.5" font-family="'Geist Mono', monospace" text-anchor="middle">256 bytes · copied by value</text>
        <text x="480" y="120" fill="#7a8399" font-size="8" font-family="'Geist Mono', monospace" text-anchor="middle">with_payload(&amp;data)</text>
        <rect x="660" y="60" width="280" height="70" rx="6" fill="rgba(235,108,54,0.08)" stroke="#eb6c36" stroke-width="1.4"/>
        <text x="800" y="88" fill="#2d3142" font-size="12" font-weight="600" font-family="'Geist', sans-serif" text-anchor="middle">inline_caps [4]</text>
        <text x="800" y="106" fill="#4f5d75" font-size="8.5" font-family="'Geist Mono', monospace" text-anchor="middle">capability ids, transferred with the message</text>
        <text x="800" y="120" fill="#7a8399" font-size="8" font-family="'Geist Mono', monospace" text-anchor="middle">with_cap(id) · MAX_INLINE_CAPS = 4</text>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <figcaption>
      Small and by-value on purpose: kernel-mediated IPC costs a copy, so keep the common case small and use
      shared memory for bulk. Capabilities travel inside the message — that's capability transfer.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Notifications: 256 bits of async signal</h2>
  <p>
    A channel is synchronous-ish (queued); a <strong>notification</strong> is "just tell me something
    happened". Each notification is four <code class="mono">AtomicU64</code> slots = 256 independent bits.
    Signalling ORs bits; waiting spins until any requested bit is set, then clears exactly those bits. It's the
    classic seL4-style signal object, and it's what you'd bind a device IRQ or an async completion to.
  </p>
<pre><code><span class="c">// ipc/notification.rs</span>
pub struct Notification &#123;
    pub id: NotificationId,
    pub process: ProcessId,
    pub slots: [AtomicU64; 4],       <span class="c">// 4 × 64 = 256 signal bits</span>
&#125;
pub fn signal(&amp;self, bits: u64) &#123;
    for (i, slot) in self.slots.iter().enumerate() &#123;
        slot.fetch_or(bits.rotate_right(i as u32 * 64), Ordering::Release);
    &#125;
&#125;
pub fn wait(&amp;self, mask: u64) -&gt; u64 &#123;
    loop &#123;
        let signaled = self.poll(mask);
        if signaled != 0 &#123; self.clear(signaled); return signaled; &#125;
        core::hint::spin_loop();      <span class="c">// a real kernel would park the task here</span>
    &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>
  <div class="callout warn">
    <span class="label">Honest gap</span>
    <code class="mono">wait()</code> spins on the CPU. With a preemptive scheduler, the correct implementation
    would mark the task <code class="mono">Blocked(Ipc)</code> and park it on a wait queue, letting the timer
    schedule someone else — the <code class="mono">BlockReason::Ipc</code> variant already exists in the task
    model. The spin also means the demo's notification is consumed by the same task that signalled it.
  </div>

  <h2>Shared memory: derive rights, share zero-copy</h2>
<pre><code><span class="c">// ipc/shared_memory.rs — narrowing a capability, not editing a permission table</span>
pub fn derive(&amp;self, rights: MemoryRights, mode: ShareMode) -&gt; Self &#123;
    let new_rights = self.rights &amp; rights;   <span class="c">// intersection: can only NARROW</span>
    self.inc_ref();
    Self &#123; frames: self.frames.clone(), rights: new_rights, share_mode: mode, .. &#125;
&#125;
<span class="c">// share_to_process() refuses if the owner tries to grant rights it doesn't hold:</span>
if !region.rights.contains(rights) &#123; return Err(ShmError::PermissionDenied); &#125;</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code class="mono">ShareMode</code> is <code class="mono">CopyOnWrite | ReadOnly | ReadWrite</code>, and the
    refcount drives destruction: <code class="mono">unmap</code> decrements; at zero the region is dropped.
    Rights can only be intersected down, never amplified — the single most important property of a capability
    system, and it's four lines here.
  </p>

  <h2>Who owns it all</h2>
  <p>
    <code class="mono">IpcManager</code> (a <code class="mono">spin::Once</code>) holds a
    <code class="mono">ChannelManager</code> (<code class="mono">RwLock&lt;BTreeMap&lt;ChannelId, Arc&lt;Mutex&lt;Channel&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;</code>),
    a <code class="mono">NotificationManager</code>, and a <code class="mono">SharedMemoryManager</code>.
    <code class="mono">ipc_demo_task</code> exercises all three at boot: it creates a channel between two
    synthetic PIDs, sends three <code class="mono">A→B</code> messages, signals and waits on a notification,
    and builds a capability table. And <code class="mono">dispatch_ipc_syscall</code> maps the
    <code class="mono">SYSCALL_IPC_*</code> numbers onto these managers for userspace.
  </p>
  <div class="callout warn">
    <span class="label">What's scaffolded</span>
    The syscall handlers currently <em>ignore</em> the user payload pointers
    (<code class="mono">_payload_ptr</code>, <code class="mono">_buffer_ptr</code>) — they create/consume messages
    with empty payloads. Shared-memory regions synthesize placeholder frames
    (<code class="mono">0, 4096, 8192…</code>) instead of asking the frame allocator, so the "zero-copy" path
    isn't wired to real pages yet. And no process owns a channel: endpoints carry a
    <code class="mono">ProcessId</code> but nothing yet maps "process X" to a live task. All three are
    integration work on a working data model.
  </div>

  <div class="qa">
    <h2>Interview Q&amp;A: IPC</h2>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">CORE</span>Why two queues per channel instead of one?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          Deadlock-free full duplex. With one queue, two processes sending simultaneously would contend, and a
          process sending a request while another sends a reply on the same queue would interleave
          nondeterministically. Two one-way queues make each direction an independent SPSC ring — which is also
          what makes the lock-free implementation legal: exactly one producer and one consumer per queue. The
          <code class="mono">call()</code> RPC operation (<code class="mono">send</code> then
          <code class="mono">recv</code>) rides the two directions as a request/reply pair.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">CORE</span>Channel vs notification vs shared memory — when do you use which?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          Channel: you need ordered messages with a reply — RPC to the filesystem or a driver. Notification: you
          need a cheap "event happened" with no payload and no ordering — an interrupt completion or a
          "buffer ready" flag, where 256 independent bits let one object serve many event sources. Shared
          memory: the payload is large and the copy cost matters — a frame buffer or a network packet ring;
          you set it up once, then both sides read/write pages directly. Real systems layer them: notify over a
          channel, then hand a shared-memory handle across.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="qa-followup"><b>FOLLOW-UP</b> "Why not always shared memory?" — Because it needs setup, lifetime management, and both sides bound to the same physical frames; and because shared writable memory is unsynchronised by construction — you still need IPC or atomics for ordering. Small control messages are cheaper as copies.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">GOTCHA</span>What prevents a process from sending on a channel it shouldn't?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          The endpoint's rights. <code class="mono">EndpointInfo</code> carries
          <code class="mono">SEND</code>/<code class="mono">RECV</code>/<code class="mono">GRANT</code>, and
          <code class="mono">send_from_a</code> checks <code class="mono">can_send()</code> first. But the
          deeper answer — and the honest one — is that this is only half-built: an endpoint has a
          <code class="mono">CapabilitySlot</code>, yet the channel manager keys by
          <code class="mono">ChannelId</code>, so there's no syscall path today that says "give endpoint b to
          process P by installing this capability in P's table". Connecting <code class="mono">CapabilityTable</code>
          slots to endpoint handles is the missing piece that would make the check secure rather than advisory.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">DESIGN</span>Why would you spin in <code class="mono">Notification::wait</code> when the kernel is preemptive?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          You wouldn't, in the end — and saying so is the right answer. <code class="mono">wait</code> is a
          correct-but-naive busy-wait; the preemptive scheduler means the spinning task is eventually switched
          out, so it doesn't hang the machine, but it burns its quantum. The proper design marks the task
          <code class="mono">Blocked(BlockReason::Ipc)</code>, appends it to a per-notification wait queue, and
          has <code class="mono">signal()</code> make it <code class="mono">Ready</code> — at which point the
          spin disappears entirely. <code class="mono">WaitSet</code> (already present) is the structure that
          would hold multi-object waits.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

